tu Lexicon

p This documentation uses a lot of expressions, most of them created \
especially for the ZPL. Words are very important in this syntax. \
You will note that every technical word is precisely chosen for describing a \
situation or an element, even for the most abstract thing.

l Here is the list of the ZPL \usymbols\u :
- Addition symbol \m+\m
-
- AND symbol \m&\m
-
- Any symbol \m?\m
-
- Assignation symbol \m=\m
-
- Division symbol \m/\m
-
- Equality symbol \m==\m
-
- Field Access symbol \m.\m
-
- Fixed collection symbol \m(,)\m
-
- Generic container symbol \m{}\m
-
- Index symbol \m[]\m
-
- Module symbol \m^.\m
-
- Modulo symbol \m%\m
-
- Negativity symbol \m-\m
-
- Opposition symbol \m~\m
-
- OR symbol \m|\m
-
- Product symbol \m*\m
-
- Range symbol \m:\m
-
- Variable collection symbol \m[,]\m
-
- XOR symbol \m^\m
-
p Not every symbol is clearly mentionned in the documentation. \
However, if one is listed, that means it has at least a \usymbolic notation\u \
associated to that symbol (especially for operators). \
This listing does not add more information about the ZPL syntax, it can only \
help you to consider the sens behind the use of one another symbol.

p As you might have noticed, the Z programming language uses trigrams for \
almost everything. Here is the list of ALL the used trigrams in Z alongside \
with their signification:

l Non-Keyword trigrams :
- ASG: Assignment
-
- BCB: Braces Begin ('{')
-
- BCE: Braces End   ('}')
-
- BKB: Brackets Begin ('[')
-
- BKE: Brackets End   (']')
-
- BLK: Blank
-
- CFG: Configuration
-
- CMC: Cascading Method Call
-
- CPL: Compiler
-
- DAT: Data
-
- DCL: Declaration
-
- EPR: Explicit Priorizing Representation
-
- EXT: External
-
- JMP: Jump
-
- LIT: Litteral
-
- LPI: Linux Programming Interface
-
- ODP: Operators Decomposition Process
-
- OOP: Object Oriented Programming
-
- PAB: Parenthesis Begin ('(')
-
- PAE: Parenthesis End   (')')
-
- PAV: Pointer As Variable
-
- PRM: Primitive
-
- RAW: Raw Text (not analyzed yet)
-
- RTY: Return Type (equivalent to TYP + void)
-
- STC: Structure
-
- STM: Statement
-
- VAP: Value Analysis Process
-
- VAV: Variable As Variable
-
- VFC: Void Function Call
-
- ZCE: Z Code Element
-
- ZCI: Z Code Instruction
-
- ZCS: Z Code Structure
-
- ZPL: Z Programming Language
-

b
l Operators trigrams :
- ADI: Arithmetic Divide
-
- AMO: Arithmetic Modulo
-
- AMU: Arithmetic Multiply
-
- BAD: B-rithmetic Add
-
- BSU: B-rithmetic Subtract
-
- CEQ: Conditionnal Equals
-
- CGT: Conditionnal Greater or Equal
-
- CGT: Conditionnal Greater Than
-
- CLE: Conditionnal Lesser or Equal
-
- CLT: Conditionnal Lesser Than
-
- CNE: Conditionnal Not Equal
-
- DAN: Decisional And
-
- DOR: Decisionnal Or
-
- FCA: Fixed Casht
-
- FFA: Fixed Field Access
-
- FRF: Fixed Reference
-
- FSZ: Fixed Size
-
- IAM: Indexing Among
-
- IIA: Indexing Index Assign
-
- IIN: Indexing Index
-
- INA: Indexing Not Among
-
- ISA: Indexing Sub Assign
-
- ISU: Indexing Sub
-
- LAN: Logical And
-
- LLB: Logical Left Byteshift
-
- LLR: Logical Left Roll
-
- LLS: Logical Left Shift
-
- LOR: Logical Or
-
- LRB: Logical Right ByteShift
-
- LRR: Logical Right Roll
-
- LRS: Logical Right Shift
-
- LXO: Logical Xor (Exclusive Or)
-
- SDC: Single Decrement
-
- SIC: Single Increment
-
- SIN: Single Invert
-
- SNO: Single Not
-

b
l Z Keywords trigrams:
- asu: asume
-
- brk: Break
-
- byt: Byte
-
- ctn: Continue
-
- cst: Constant
-
- dbl: Double
-
- def: Default
-
- elf: elif (which is itself a contraction of "else if")
-
- els: else
-
- enm: Enumerated
-
- fct: Function
-
- flt: Float
-
- fwd: Forward
-
- imp: Import
-
- int: Integer
-
- lng: Long
-
- lnk: Link
-
- mod: Module
-
- ptr: Pointer
-
- ret: Return
-
- shr: Short
-
- swi: Switch
-
- typ: Type
-
- voi: Void (funtion return type)
-
- whi: While
-

b
l Other very common trigrams used in Z programs:
- boo : Boolean
-
- chr : Character
-
- lst : List (simple linked list to be precise)
-
- map : Map
-
- str : String
-
- tab : Table
-
- unt : Unit
-





tu Last Note

l I will not be very long on this last note :

p Please consider that the Z programming language is a FREE and OPEN SOURCE \
product. This language including its concept, standard library, compiler and \
this documentation took an important amount of time, energy and thinking to be \
achieved.

lc Please respect the work done.

b

